# The ledger of `mail`: what Go's `net/mail`, `mime`, `mime/multipart` and
# `mime/quotedprintable` (Go 1.27), Python's `email` with its `message`,
# `policy`, `headerregistry`, `utils`, `parser`, `generator`, `header`,
# `charset`, `encoders`, `errors`, `iterators`, `contentmanager`,
# `quoprimime`, `base64mime` and `mime` modules, `quopri` and `mimetypes`
# (3.15), and Rust's `lettre` (its message side) and `mail-parser` let a
# program do, one block per capability, and what Rebel answers. Sending is
# `mail/smtp`'s ledger. The lists are this machine's, 2026-11-17: `go doc
# -all` of the four Go packages, `dir()` of each Python module and of
# `EmailMessage`, `EmailPolicy`, `Address`, `Group`, `BaseHeader`, `Header`
# and `Charset`; Rust's standard library has no mail, so its line names the
# two crates the brief gives as the yardstick, their items read from
# docs.rs's `all.html` the same day (neither crate is on this machine). Every
# expected value of the package's tests that Go or Python answers was
# observed from them the same day.

capability message/read  a message read from bytes: its header fields in order, its body
  heroes  mail.decode_message
  go      net/mail.ReadMessage, Message, Message.Header, Message.Body
  rust    mail_parser::MessageParser, mail_parser::Message, mail_parser::MessageRef
  python  email.message_from_bytes, email.message_from_string, email.parser.BytesParser, email.parser.Parser, email.parser.FeedParser, email.parser.BytesFeedParser, email.message.EmailMessage, email.message.Message, email.message.MIMEPart
  note    never fails, as Python reads: a line that is no field ends the header (Python's MissingHeaderBodySeparatorDefect), and what was repaired is told in `defects`; Go fails on such a line. A header byte that is not UTF-8 is read as ISO-8859-1, where Python keeps it with surrogate escapes and Go keeps the bytes

capability message/read-stream  a message read from a stream or a file
  heroes  mail.read_message
  go      net/mail.ReadMessage
  rust    -
  python  email.message_from_binary_file, email.message_from_file, email.parser.BytesParser.parse, email.parser.Parser.parse
  note    the stream is read to its end and parsed whole

capability message/defects  what was wrong in a message read, told and not raised
  heroes  mail.Message.defects
  go      -
  rust    mail_parser::DecodeProblems
  python  email.message.EmailMessage.defects, email.headerregistry.BaseHeader.defects, email.policy.EmailPolicy.handle_defect, email.policy.EmailPolicy.register_defect, email.policy.EmailPolicy.raise_on_defect, email.policy.strict, email.errors.MessageDefect, email.errors.NoBoundaryInMultipartDefect, email.errors.StartBoundaryNotFoundDefect, email.errors.CloseBoundaryNotFoundDefect, email.errors.FirstHeaderLineIsContinuationDefect, email.errors.MisplacedEnvelopeHeaderDefect, email.errors.MissingHeaderBodySeparatorDefect, email.errors.MultipartInvariantViolationDefect, email.errors.InvalidMultipartContentTransferEncodingDefect, email.errors.UndecodableBytesDefect, email.errors.InvalidBase64PaddingDefect, email.errors.InvalidBase64CharactersDefect, email.errors.InvalidBase64LengthDefect, email.errors.HeaderDefect, email.errors.InvalidHeaderDefect, email.errors.HeaderMissingRequiredValue, email.errors.NonPrintableDefect, email.errors.ObsoleteHeaderDefect, email.errors.NonASCIILocalPartDefect, email.errors.InvalidDateDefect
  note    a sentence each; a strict reader is a program that refuses a message whose `defects` is not empty, where Python's `raise_on_defect` raises

capability message/write  a message written as bytes, its header folded, its parts with boundaries
  heroes  mail.encode_message, mail.write_message
  go      -
  rust    lettre::Message, lettre::message::Message
  python  email.message.EmailMessage.as_bytes, email.message.EmailMessage.as_string, email.generator.BytesGenerator, email.generator.Generator, email.policy.EmailPolicy.linesep, email.policy.EmailPolicy.max_line_length, email.policy.EmailPolicy.verify_generated_headers, email.errors.HeaderWriteError
  note    a value holding a line break is refused `invalid`, as Python's `verify_generated_headers` refuses one, against header injection; a body's line breaks are written as the options' line end but under `binary`, as Python's generator writes them

capability message/write-options  the line end, the fold length and UTF-8 in the header chosen
  heroes  mail.WriteOptions, mail.standard_options, mail.utf8_options
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  email.policy.SMTP, email.policy.SMTPUTF8, email.policy.HTTP, email.policy.default, email.policy.EmailPolicy, email.policy.EmailPolicy.clone, email.policy.EmailPolicy.utf8, email.policy.EmailPolicy.linesep, email.policy.EmailPolicy.max_line_length, email.policy.EmailPolicy.cte_type, email.policy.compat32
  note    Python's `HTTP` policy is `line_length: 0`; `cte_type` is the builders' choice, always 7-bit (below); Python's compat32 behaviour, the old API kept for old programs, has no counterpart

capability header/read  the header fields alone, unfolded
  heroes  mail.decode_fields
  go      -
  rust    mail_parser::Headers, mail_parser::Header, mail_parser::HeaderIter, mail_parser::HeaderForm, mail_parser::HeaderName, mail_parser::HeaderKey, mail_parser::NamedHeaders
  python  email.parser.BytesHeaderParser, email.parser.HeaderParser, email.message.EmailMessage.raw_items, email.policy.EmailPolicy.header_source_parse
  note    a value is kept as written, unfolded, and never decoded: decoding is asked (`decode_header`, `parse_address_list`, `parse_date`), where Python's default policy decodes on reading

capability header/write  a header field folded to a line length, the header written
  heroes  mail.format_field, mail.encode_fields
  go      -
  rust    lettre::message::header::Headers, lettre::message::header::HeaderName, lettre::message::header::HeaderValue, lettre::message::header::InvalidHeaderName
  python  email.policy.EmailPolicy.fold, email.policy.EmailPolicy.fold_binary, email.headerregistry.BaseHeader.fold, email.policy.EmailPolicy.refold_source
  note    folded before a space; a word longer than the line stays whole, as Python leaves it

capability header/unfold  a folded value made one line
  heroes  mail.unfold
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  email.policy.EmailPolicy.header_fetch_parse
  note    every line break before a space or a tab is removed, the space kept (RFC 5322 § 2.2.3)

capability header/lookup  a field's value by its name in any case, every value of a name, whether one is there
  heroes  mail.value_of, mail.values_of, mail.has_field
  go      net/mail.Header, net/mail.Header.Get, net/mail.ErrHeaderNotPresent
  rust    mail_parser::Message
  python  email.message.EmailMessage.get, email.message.EmailMessage.get_all, email.message.EmailMessage.keys, email.message.EmailMessage.values, email.message.EmailMessage.items
  note    absent is `not_found`, where Go answers "" and Python None; the fields themselves are `Message.fields`, in order

capability header/change  a field set, added, removed
  heroes  mail.with_field, mail.with_field_added, mail.without_field
  go      -
  rust    lettre::message::MessageBuilder, lettre::message::header::Headers
  python  email.message.EmailMessage.add_header, email.message.EmailMessage.replace_header, email.message.EmailMessage.set_raw, email.message.EmailMessage.clear, email.policy.EmailPolicy.header_store_parse, email.policy.EmailPolicy.header_max_count
  note    `with_field` keeps one field of the name, so a second `Subject` is never written by mistake (Python's `header_max_count`); `with_field_added` adds one beside the others, `Received` and `Comments`

capability header/typed  a field read as what it holds: addresses, a date, a content type, a disposition, a transfer encoding, identifiers
  heroes  mail.addresses_of, mail.date_of, mail.content_type_of, mail.disposition_of, mail.transfer_encoding_of, mail.parse_message_ids
  go      net/mail.Header.AddressList, net/mail.Header.Date
  rust    mail_parser::HeaderValue, mail_parser::Message, mail_parser::ParsedValue, mail_parser::TextList
  python  email.headerregistry.HeaderRegistry, email.headerregistry.AddressHeader, email.headerregistry.SingleAddressHeader, email.headerregistry.UniqueAddressHeader, email.headerregistry.UniqueSingleAddressHeader, email.headerregistry.DateHeader, email.headerregistry.UniqueDateHeader, email.headerregistry.UnstructuredHeader, email.headerregistry.UniqueUnstructuredHeader, email.headerregistry.ContentTypeHeader, email.headerregistry.ContentDispositionHeader, email.headerregistry.ContentTransferEncodingHeader, email.headerregistry.ParameterizedMIMEHeader, email.headerregistry.MIMEVersionHeader, email.headerregistry.MessageIDHeader, email.headerregistry.ReferencesHeader, email.headerregistry.BaseHeader, email.headerregistry.BaseHeader.name, email.headerregistry.BaseHeader.init, email.policy.EmailPolicy.header_factory
  note    a function per kind of field, where Python's registry makes each header an object; `MIME-Version` is read with `value_of`

capability mailbox/parse  one mailbox read: a name, a quoted local part, a domain literal, comments, encoded words in the name
  heroes  mail.parse_address, mail.Address
  go      net/mail.ParseAddress, net/mail.Address, net/mail.Address.Name, net/mail.Address.Address, net/mail.AddressParser, net/mail.AddressParser.Parse, net/mail.AddressParser.WordDecoder
  rust    lettre::message::Mailbox, lettre::Address, lettre::address::Address, lettre::address::AddressError, mail_parser::Mailbox, mail_parser::parse_address_local_part, mail_parser::parse_address_domain
  python  email.headerregistry.Address, email.headerregistry.Address.display_name, email.headerregistry.Address.username, email.headerregistry.Address.domain, email.headerregistry.Address.addr_spec, email.utils.parseaddr
  note    refused as Go refuses them: no `@`, a dot at an end of a local part or two together (Python reads those with a defect); a comment between the local part and `@` is skipped as RFC 5322 allows (Go refuses it); a comment after a bare address is taken as its name where none is written, as Go and `parseaddr` do; an encoded word holding a comma is read whole, as Python reads it

capability address/list  an address list read, groups flattened or kept
  heroes  mail.parse_address_list, mail.parse_recipients, mail.Recipient
  go      net/mail.ParseAddressList, net/mail.AddressParser.ParseList
  rust    lettre::message::Mailboxes, lettre::message::header::MailboxesHeader, mail_parser::Address, mail_parser::AddressList, mail_parser::AddressRun, mail_parser::Group
  python  email.utils.getaddresses, email.headerregistry.Group, email.headerregistry.Group.addresses, email.headerregistry.Group.display_name, email.headerregistry.AddressHeader.addresses, email.headerregistry.AddressHeader.groups
  note    empty entries between commas are skipped (RFC 5322's obs-addr-list); an entry that is no mailbox fails the whole list `invalid`, as Go fails it, where `getaddresses` answers ('', '')

capability address/write  a mailbox and a list written, names quoted or encoded where they must be
  heroes  mail.format_address, mail.format_address_list, mail.format_recipients, mail.format_addr_spec
  go      net/mail.Address.String
  rust    lettre::message::Mailbox, lettre::message::Mailboxes
  python  email.utils.formataddr, email.headerregistry.Address.__str__, email.utils.quote, email.utils.unquote, email.utils.COMMASPACE, email.utils.specialsre, email.utils.escapesre
  note    a name that is atoms is written bare, as Python writes it (Go quotes every name); a name above ASCII as Q-encoded words limited to the bytes RFC 2047 § 5 lets a phrase hold, where Go also leaves `,` and `.` unencoded; an address with no name is written bare, as Python writes it, where Go writes it in angle brackets

capability address/utf8  an address above ASCII (RFC 6532), read, written, and told apart for SMTPUTF8
  heroes  mail.is_ascii_address, mail.utf8_options
  go      net/mail.ParseAddress
  rust    -
  python  email.policy.SMTPUTF8, email.errors.NonASCIILocalPartDefect
  note    a local part or a domain above ASCII is read and written as UTF-8; with `standard_options` a domain is written as its A-labels (address/idna) and a local part fails `unsupported`, where Python writes it raw and only the server refuses

capability address/idna  a domain above ASCII written in ASCII (IDNA's A-labels)
  heroes  mail.encode_message, mail.encode_fields, mail.format_field, url/idna.to_ascii
  go      -
  rust    lettre::Address
  python  -
  note    a message written with no UTF-8 allowed writes an address's domain above ASCII by `url/idna.to_ascii` with `strict_options`, every check of a name looked up in DNS, `bücher.de` as `xn--bcher-kva.de`; a local part above ASCII has no ASCII form and fails `unsupported`; with UTF-8 allowed (RFC 6532) the domain is written as it is

capability address/subaddress  the detail after `+` in a local part
  heroes  refused: a convention of some servers (RFC 5233 names it for Sieve), not of a message; `strings.split_once(s: a.local, sep: "+")` answers it
  go      -
  rust    mail_parser::parse_address_detail_part, mail_parser::parse_address_user_part
  python  -

capability words/decode  encoded words (RFC 2047) decoded, B and Q, a charset this package reads
  heroes  mail.decode_header
  go      mime.WordDecoder, mime.WordDecoder.Decode, mime.WordDecoder.DecodeHeader
  rust    mail_parser::Encoding
  python  email.header.decode_header, email.header.make_header, email.header.ecre, email.header.fcre
  note    space between two words is dropped, a word not well formed stays text (as Go reads it; Python decodes broken base 64 to nothing), base 64 missing its padding is read (Python), an RFC 2231 language after `*` in the charset is read (Python; Go fails); a UTF-8 word whose bytes are not UTF-8 fails `not_text`, where Go keeps the bytes and Python writes U+FFFD

capability words/keep-bytes  encoded words in a charset not read kept as bytes, and said so
  heroes  mail.decode_words, mail.Word
  go      mime.WordDecoder.CharsetReader
  rust    mail_parser::decoders::charsets::DecoderFnc
  python  email.header.decode_header
  note    adjacent words of one charset are joined before their bytes are decoded, so a character split across two is read whole, as Python joins them; Go decodes each alone and fails there. A program brings its own decoder by reading `data` and `charset`, where Go takes a function

capability words/encode  text written as encoded words that never split a character, each at most 75 bytes
  heroes  mail.encode_words, mail.WordEncoding
  go      mime.WordEncoder, mime.WordEncoder.Encode, mime.BEncoding, mime.QEncoding
  rust    -
  python  email.header.Header, email.header.Header.encode, email.header.Header.append, email.header.MAXLINELEN, email.header.USASCII, email.header.UTF8, email.header.SPACE, email.header.SPACE8, email.header.FWS, email.header.BSPACE, email.header.NL, email.header.EMPTYSTRING
  note    UTF-8 only, as Go writes; the words are Go's byte for byte (observed). Writing a header with `standard_options` encodes the words from the first above ASCII to the last as one run, Q unless B is five bytes shorter, as Python's policy does (observed)

capability charsets/read  the charsets of words, parameters and parts decoded
  heroes  mail.text_of, mail.decode_header
  go      -
  rust    mail_parser::decoders::charsets::Charset, mail_parser::decoders::charsets::charset_decoder, mail_parser::decoders::charsets::decode, mail_parser::decoders::charsets::decode_append
  python  email.charset.Charset, email.charset.Charset.get_output_charset, email.charset.ALIASES, email.charset.CHARSETS, email.charset.CODEC_MAP, email.charset.DEFAULT_CHARSET, email.charset.UNKNOWN8BIT, email.message.EmailMessage.get_charsets, email.message.EmailMessage.get_content_charset
  note    UTF-8, US-ASCII and ISO-8859-1 by their names and aliases, read here; every other WHATWG encoding (Windows-1252, the ISO-8859 family, Shift_JIS, EUC-JP, GB18030, Big5, EUC-KR, KOI8) through `unicode/encodings` (charsets/legacy). A charset no encoding names fails `unsupported` and `decode_words` keeps its bytes. The charset of a part is `content_type_of(m).params["charset"]`

capability charsets/legacy  the WHATWG legacy encodings read in mail
  heroes  mail.text_of, mail.decode_header, mail.decode_words, unicode/encodings.decode_strict
  go      -
  rust    mail_parser::decoders::charsets::Charset
  python  email.charset.Charset, email.charset.CODEC_MAP
  note    every label `unicode/encodings.name_of` reads, decoded strictly, a byte the encoding cannot read failing `not_text` as a byte not UTF-8 does; ISO-8859-1 stays Latin-1, each byte the character of its number, and US-ASCII refuses a byte above 127, where WHATWG reads both labels as Windows-1252; the labels WHATWG reads as `replacement` (ISO-2022-KR, ISO-2022-CN, HZ) fail `unsupported`, their bytes kept, as Python's `email` reads them and WHATWG refuses them

capability charsets/register  a program's own charset or alias registered
  heroes  refused: a registry is a mutable global, which Heroes has not; `decode_words` hands a program the bytes and the charset's name to decode itself
  go      mime.WordDecoder.CharsetReader
  rust    -
  python  email.charset.add_alias, email.charset.add_charset, email.charset.add_codec

capability mime/content-type  a content type read and written with its parameters
  heroes  mail.parse_content_type, mail.format_content_type, mail.ContentType, mail.content_type_of
  go      mime.ParseMediaType, mime.FormatMediaType, mime.ErrInvalidMediaParameter
  rust    lettre::message::header::ContentType, lettre::message::header::ContentTypeErr, mail_parser::ContentType
  python  email.message.EmailMessage.get_content_type, email.message.EmailMessage.get_content_maintype, email.message.EmailMessage.get_content_subtype, email.message.EmailMessage.get_param, email.message.EmailMessage.get_params, email.message.EmailMessage.set_param, email.message.EmailMessage.del_param, email.message.EmailMessage.set_type, email.message.EmailMessage.get_boundary, email.message.EmailMessage.set_boundary, email.message.EmailMessage.set_charset, email.message.EmailMessage.get_charset, email.message.SEMISPACE, email.message.tspecials
  note    a parameter twice is refused, as Go refuses it; an unquoted value above ASCII is read, as Python reads it (Go refuses); a part with no valid content type is `text/plain; charset=us-ascii`, RFC 2045's, as Python answers. A parameter is changed by changing `params` and writing the field with `with_field`

capability mime/default-type  the content type a part of a `multipart/digest` has when it names none
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  email.message.EmailMessage.get_default_type, email.message.EmailMessage.set_default_type
  note    the default is the parent's to give, and a `Message` holds no parent; `content_type_of` answers `text/plain` for such a part, which its doc says

capability mime/rfc2231  parameters by RFC 2231: charsets, languages and continuations, read and written
  heroes  mail.parse_content_type, mail.parse_disposition, mail.format_content_type, mail.format_disposition
  go      mime.ParseMediaType, mime.FormatMediaType
  rust    -
  python  email.utils.decode_rfc2231, email.utils.encode_rfc2231, email.utils.collapse_rfc2231_value, email.utils.decode_params, email.utils.rfc2231_continuation, email.utils.TICK
  note    continuations are joined while each number is there, as Go stops at a gap; a value in a charset not read is kept as written under its name with `*`, where Go drops it and Python keeps it raw; a long value above ASCII is written in continuations, as Python writes it, where Go writes one long piece

capability mime/disposition  a disposition read and written, a part's file name, whether it is an attachment
  heroes  mail.parse_disposition, mail.format_disposition, mail.Disposition, mail.disposition_of, mail.filename_of, mail.is_attachment
  go      mime/multipart.Part.FileName
  rust    lettre::message::header::ContentDisposition, mail_parser::MessagePart, mail_parser::PartFlags
