  note    the file name is the disposition's `filename`, else the content type's `name`, as Python looks; encoded words in it are decoded, as mail readers do for the many writers that put them there against RFC 2047

capability mime/tree  a multipart read as a tree, its preamble and epilogue kept
  heroes  mail.decode_message, mail.Message.parts, mail.Message.preamble, mail.Message.epilogue, mail.is_multipart, mail.MAX_DEPTH
  go      mime/multipart.NewReader, mime/multipart.Reader, mime/multipart.Reader.NextPart, mime/multipart.Reader.NextRawPart, mime/multipart.Part, mime/multipart.Part.Header, mime/multipart.Part.Read, mime/multipart.Part.Close
  rust    mail_parser::MessagePart, mail_parser::PartKind, mail_parser::PartId, mail_parser::Message
  python  email.message.EmailMessage.is_multipart, email.message.EmailMessage.iter_parts, email.message.EmailMessage.get_payload, email.message.EmailMessage.preamble, email.message.EmailMessage.epilogue, email.message.EmailMessage.attach, email.message.EmailMessage.set_payload
  note    read with an explicit stack, never recursion, and 64 levels deep at most, a deeper multipart kept as one part with a defect; a boundary line may carry spaces after it (RFC 2046's transport padding), and a missing closing boundary ends the last part at the end, with a defect, as Python reads it. Go reads one level as a stream; a program reading a part's bytes asks `content_of`

